prompt:
Independently hostile-review the read-only ViceSharp requirements recovery findings and the proposed realignment plan. Execute the complete add-profile skill first. Classify this as project planning and requirements work. Do not modify product code, MCP requirements, TODO state, or existing plans. You may create only the required response JSONL receipt.

Validate all four surfaces:
A. Requested result: Are the audit-derived counts and causal claims supported? Key claims to verify are: pre-collapse commit d28493a contains 138 unique FR/TR/TEST records; 112 are absent from live MCP; current canonical docs contain 204 unique IDs and 125 are absent from live MCP; live MCP contains 383 records, 381 unique IDs, 293 records with zero acceptance criteria, two cross-type duplicate IDs, 25 unmapped FRs, and 115 internally valid mappings; May 27 export was generated by MCP from an incomplete DB; no explicit ID-reuse rationale is recorded.
B. Workspace/process compliance: read-only recovery, PowerShell only, no requirements/code mutation, UWP/Xbox excluded from active scope, receipts and observations distinguished from inference.
C. Requirements correctness: observable emulator behavior is FR; architecture is TR; executable VICE differential proof is TEST; restore broad FR-CPU-002, FR-VIC-001..010, broad TR-CYCLE-001, current canonical VIC-20 FRs, and preserve later legitimate requirements without destructive replacement. Check whether the plan correctly treats VICE as executable oracle plus hardware evidence.
D. Plan completeness: evaluate this decision-complete phase plan:
0 freeze/snapshot live MCP, Git, TODO audit, session audit, and native VICE ref; no generate/ingest;
1 build a per-ID recovery ledger across initial commit d1f7175, pre-collapse d28493a, current canonical docs, live MCP, TODO/session audit; classify restore/merge/re-ID/retire/owner-decision;
2 restore requirements only after approval, with broad foundational IDs restored, narrow checkpoint language moved into AC/TR/TEST, placeholder technical TEST duplicates removed, statuses reset to pending until proven, Xbox/UWP retired from active scope without erasing history;
3 require concrete ACs and FR to TR to TEST mapping for every active behavior, eliminate zero-AC and unmapped active records, and prevent authoritative import deletion;
4 audit managed code subsystem-by-subsystem against pinned x64sc/xvic source and seed with PLAN-VICEPARITY-001's 47 findings plus the 2026-09-28 CPU architecture audit;
5 execute BDPv4 slices in dependency order with hostile gates after requirements, red tests, implementation, and full green;
6 prove x64sc/xvic lockstep with fail-closed native prerequisites, full per-cycle bus/device state, cold boot and real workloads, PAL/NTSC, media/input/snapshot/reset, no silent returns/skips/pending in required scope;
7 implement and prove Avalonia family-based settings visibility with behavioral PropertyChanged and live UI tests, excluding Xbox/UWP;
8 regenerate docs only from reconciled MCP after a deletion/diff guard, run traceability/full suites/hostile final review, then commit/push only after approval and green evidence.

Attack known gaps: current long xvic tests return early and therefore pass when the oracle/env is absent; lockstep mostly compares CPU regs plus video state rather than complete bus/device effects; SettingsView uses IsVic20Selected but SelectedMachineProfile setter does not raise PropertyChanged for it; source-text UI tests are not behavioral proof. Determine whether any plan phase, acceptance gate, source category, or rollback/merge protection is missing.

Write the full response as JSONL to F:\GitHub\vice-sharp\docs\receipts\hv\20260928T184743Z-requirements-recovery-realignment-plan.response.jsonl. The final object must include OverallVerdict AGREE or DISAGREE, numeric accuracyScore and completenessScore, PASS/FAIL/UNKNOWN findings, all source anchors and commands, and persistence proof. Both scores must be at least 98 for AGREE. Create and complete a dedicated MCP Session Log turn, persist the entire verdict body there, then query it back.
